Q: ASP.NET Core DI error: Unable to resolve service for type StackExchange.Redis.IDatabase A: <p>Alternative solution without registering the <code>IDatabase</code> service in the DI container.</p>
<ol>
<li>In your <code>RateLimiterService</code> (or any service that uses Redis) constructor, obtain the <code>IConnectionMultiplexer</code> service instead of <code>IDatabase</code> service from DI container.</li>
<li>Get the <code>IDatabase</code> service instance via <code>redis.GetDatabase()</code>.</li>
</ol>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>public class RateLimiterService
{
    private readonly IDatabase _db;

    public RateLimiterService(IConnectionMultiplexer redis)
    {
        _db = redis.GetDatabase();
    }

    ...
}
</code></pre>
<p>Reference: <a href="https://redis.io/tutorials/develop/dotnet/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">.NET and Redis Tutorial: StackExchange.Redis Getting Started Guide</a></p>

Q: Defining Function Types in C A: <p>In this reply, I am assuming that the data type of the two function arguments and of the return value are always the same. For example, I am assuming that if the two arguments are of type <code>double</code>, then the return value is also of type <code>double</code>.</p>
<p>In C, the best you can do is</p>
<ol>
<li>to pass a data structure to the function that is able to represent all possible data types that you want, and to return such a data structure, or</li>
<li>to define a function for every possible type that you want to use, and then, when calling the function, use <a href="https://en.cppreference.com/c/language/generic" rel="nofollow noreferrer">generic selection</a> so that the appropriate function for the type is automatically called.</li>
</ol>
<p>Here is a demonstration program for #1:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

enum
{
    DATA_TYPE_INT,
    DATA_TYPE_DOUBLE,

    // this value must be last in the list
    DATA_TYPE_INVALID
};

typedef struct
{
    unsigned char type;
    union
    {
        int i;
        double d;
    };

} any_type;

// This function will create a value of type &quot;any_type&quot; from an &quot;int&quot;.
any_type create_int( int value )
{
    return (any_type){ DATA_TYPE_INT, .i = value };
}

// This function will create a value of type &quot;any_type&quot; from a &quot;double&quot;.
any_type create_double( double value )
{
    return (any_type){ DATA_TYPE_DOUBLE, .d = value };
}

// This function will halve the value passed to it.
any_type halve_value( any_type value )
{
    switch ( value.type )
    {
        case DATA_TYPE_INT:
            value.i /= 2;
            break;

        case DATA_TYPE_DOUBLE:
            value.d /= 2;
            break;

        default:
            printf( &quot;WARNING: halve operation failed due to invalid data type!\n&quot; );
            return (any_type){ DATA_TYPE_INVALID };
    }

    return value;
}

void print_value( any_type value )
{
    switch ( value.type )
    {
        case DATA_TYPE_INT:
            printf
            (
                &quot;The type is \&quot;int\&quot;.\n&quot;
                &quot;The value is: %d\n&quot;,
                value.i
            );
            break;

        case DATA_TYPE_DOUBLE:
            printf
            (
                &quot;The type is \&quot;double\&quot;.\n&quot;
                &quot;The value is: %f\n&quot;,
                value.d
            );
            break;

        default:
            printf( &quot;The type is INVALID.\n&quot; );
    }
}

int main( void )
{
    any_type i = create_int( 123 );
    any_type d = create_double( 456.78 );

    // print both values before halving
    printf( &quot;VALUES BEFORE HALVE OPERATION:\n\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;First value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( i );
    printf( &quot;\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;Second value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( d );
    printf( &quot;\n\n&quot; );

    // halve both values
    i = halve_value( i );
    d = halve_value( d );

    // print both values after halving
    printf( &quot;VALUES AFTER HALVE OPERATION:\n\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;First value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( i );
    printf( &quot;\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;Second value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( d );
    printf( &quot;\n\n&quot; );
}
</code></pre>
<p>This program has the following output:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>VALUES BEFORE HALVE OPERATION:

First value:
The type is &quot;int&quot;.
The value is: 123

Second value:
The type is &quot;double&quot;.
The value is: 456.780000


VALUES AFTER HALVE OPERATION:

First value:
The type is &quot;int&quot;.
The value is: 61

Second value:
The type is &quot;double&quot;.
The value is: 228.390000
</code></pre>
<p>Here is a demonstration program for #2:</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

#define halve_value( X ) \
    _Generic \
    ( \
        ( X ), \
        int:     halve_value_int, \
        double:  halve_value_double \
    ) \
    ( X )

#define print_value( X ) \
    _Generic \
    ( \
        ( X ), \
        int:     print_value_int, \
        double:  print_value_double \
    ) \
    ( X )

int halve_value_int( int value )
{
    return value /= 2;
}

double halve_value_double( double value )
{
    return value /= 2;
}

void print_value_int( int value )
{
    printf
    (
        &quot;The type is \&quot;int\&quot;.\n&quot;
        &quot;The value is: %d\n&quot;,
        value
    );
}

void print_value_double( double value )
{
    printf
    (
        &quot;The type is \&quot;double\&quot;.\n&quot;
        &quot;The value is: %f\n&quot;,
        value
    );
}

int main( void )
{
    int i    = 123;
    double d = 456.78;

    // print both values before halving
    printf( &quot;VALUES BEFORE HALVE OPERATION:\n\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;First value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( i );
    printf( &quot;\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;Second value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( d );
    printf( &quot;\n\n&quot; );

    // halve both values
    i = halve_value( i );
    d = halve_value( d );

    // print both values after halving
    printf( &quot;VALUES AFTER HALVE OPERATION:\n\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;First value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( i );
    printf( &quot;\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;Second value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( d );
    printf( &quot;\n\n&quot; );
}
</code></pre>
<p>This demonstration program has the same output as the first one.</p>
<p>However, if you want a C-like programming language with <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Function_overloading" rel="nofollow noreferrer">function overloading</a> and <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Template_(C%2B%2B)" rel="nofollow noreferrer">templates</a> built into the core language, then I recommend that you take a look at the programming language C++. This language allows you to do the following:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;type_traits&gt;

template &lt;typename T&gt;
T halve_value( T value )
{
    return value / 2;
}

template &lt;typename T&gt;
void print_value( T value )
{
    if ( std::is_same&lt;T, int&gt;::value )
    {
        printf
        (
            &quot;The type is \&quot;int\&quot;.\n&quot;
            &quot;The value is: %d\n&quot;,
            value
        );
    }    
    else if ( std::is_same&lt;T, double&gt;::value )
    {
        printf
        (
            &quot;The type is \&quot;double\&quot;.\n&quot;
            &quot;The value is: %f\n&quot;,
            value
        );
    }
    else
    {
        printf( &quot;The type is not int or double.\n&quot; );
    }
}

int main()
{
    int i    = 123;
    double d = 456.78;

    // print both values before halving
    printf( &quot;VALUES BEFORE HALVE OPERATION:\n\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;First value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( i );
    printf( &quot;\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;Second value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( d );
    printf( &quot;\n\n&quot; );

    // halve both values
    i = halve_value( i );
    d = halve_value( d );

    // print both values after halving
    printf( &quot;VALUES AFTER HALVE OPERATION:\n\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;First value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( i );
    printf( &quot;\n&quot; );
    printf( &quot;Second value:\n&quot; );
    print_value( d );
    printf( &quot;\n\n&quot; );
}
</code></pre>
<p>This program also has the same output as the other two demonstration programs.</p>
<p>Note that most C++ programmers would not use the function <code>printf</code>, as that is considered bad style in C++. They would rather use a function that is part of the C++ standard library, which allows you to print in a type-generic way. However, for consistency with the other two examples, I also used the function <code>printf</code> in the C++ demonstration program.</p>

Q: messaging.onMessage() (JavaScript) is not being called when a push notification is sent A: <p>The whole concept of push notifications is made more confusing, because there are actually different web APIs involved which work together and this is often not made clear. Then there is the debate over should you use VAPID or FCM? How about VAPID with FCM? Blah blah blah.</p>
<p>So, first off, you need to handle checking / getting user permission for notifications, creating / updating the subscription, saving the token etc. If you are getting a token / subscription then you are all set to send data to the client (this is Push API stuff).</p>
<p>Technically, this is all you need if your users never leave your website, but obviously they will so this is why you need a service worker. The service worker can receive your push data without the user having your site open and then do something with it. (This is Push/Service Worker API crossover).</p>
<p>Finally, you want your users to see whatever you sent them a message about, right? The Push API gets your message data to your service worker but you still have to handle that data. You probably want to display a notification, so you need the Notification API.</p>
<p>Now, looking at your code, you seem to be doing the first part in your front end and getting a token for your users, but your service worker contains literally no code, so it does nothing. I know Firebase has some custom event handlers, but standard web push ones should work too.</p>
<p>All you need to get started is a basic handler for push events in your service worker code, like this:</p>
<pre><code>self.addEventListener('push', function(event) {
    const push = event.data.json();
    const title = push.data.title;
    const options = JSON.parse(push.data.options);
    event.waitUntil(registration.showNotification(title, options));
});
</code></pre>
<p>The nice thing about this is by keeping the code simple and not hard coding any of the options for the notification within your service worker it allows your server side code to customise the behaviour and appearance of each notification on the fly. The service worker is literally just parsing the JSON input of your push payload and creating a notification using the options within the payload</p>
<p>You can find lists of the available options for the Notification API <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/notification" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a>, they are still changing, but as of now, the following PHP example in conjunction with the service worker code above will send a fairly fully featured notification via Firebase:</p>
<pre><code>$push['to'] = $SUBSCRIBED-FCM-TOKEN;
$push['data']['title'] = 'Here is my title';
$push['data']['options']['body'] = 'and here is the body message';
$push['data']['options']['badge'] = '/images/chaticon.png';
$push['data']['options']['requireInteraction'] = true;
$push['data']['options']['tag'] = 'notification-tag';
$push['data']['options']['icon'] = '/images/icons/256.jpg';
$push['data']['options']['vibrate'] = array(500,150,500,150,500);
$push['data']['options']['image'] = '/images/logo.jpg';
$push['data']['options']['actions'] = array(array('action' =&gt; 'accept-action', 'title' =&gt; 'Yes', 'icon' =&gt; '/images/icons/yes.jpg' ), array('action' =&gt; 'reject-action', 'title' =&gt; 'No', 'icon' =&gt; '/images/icons/no.jpg'));
$ch = curl_init();
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_URL, &quot;https://fcm.googleapis.com/fcm/send&quot;);
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_RETURNTRANSFER, 1);
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_POSTFIELDS, json_encode($push));
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_POST, 1);
$headers = array();
$headers[] = &quot;Content-Type: application/json&quot;;
$headers[] = &quot;Authorization: key=$SERVER-KEY-FROM-FIREBASE-CONSOLE&quot;;
curl_setopt($ch, CURLOPT_HTTPHEADER, $headers);
$result = curl_exec($ch);
curl_close ($ch);
</code></pre>
<p>Obviously, this is just a basic example to get you going. You may choose to add fetch events in response to clicks on your notification actions, etc. to perform further actions, for analytics or whatever. You can also alter the behaviour based upon whether the user is already on your site or not.</p>
